r#  9952  194  20
p: .
t. 
|  Research networks or grids are an increasingly popular
|  architecture for collaborative and decentralized
.  science communities


--- Sentence/switch
id: 49
r#  9953  194  21


--- Sentence//end
id: 49
r#  10337  200  110
p: .
t. 
|   Examples include the
|  Cancer Bioinformatics Grid (caBIG),
|  Center for the Development of
|  a Virtual Tumor (CVIT), National Cancer Informatics Program (NCIP),
|  Data Infrastructure Building Blocks (from the
|  National Science Foundation), Open Science Grid (OSG),
.  Worldwide LHC Computing Grid (WLCG), EarthCube, Earth System Grid Federation (ESGF), and many others


--- Sentence/switch
id: 50
r#  10338  200  111

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 21
y: N/A


--- Paragraph/start
id: 22
r#  10800  209  1


--- Sentence/start
id: 51
r#  10800  209  1

--- Sentence//end
id: 51
r#  10859  209  60
p: .
t. 
|  Although originally designed to support decentralized
|  parallel computing  -  that is, for original
|  research  -  grids have evolved into
|  pedagogical and reproduction tools as well
|  as hosts for initial experiments.\footnote{\raggedright\citeref{\q{%
|  caGrid 1.0: An Enterprise Grid Infrastructure for Biomedical Research} (2008)} %
.  \hspace*{19pt}(\href{https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2274794/}{https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2274794/})}Grid specifications are often focused on metadata standards


--- Sentence/switch
id: 52
r#  10860  209  61


--- Sentence//end
id: 52
r#  11026  212  41
p: .
t. 
|  There are not necessarily standard guidelines for actual
|  code, as written in common science-computing languages, for
.  components that will be hosted on a grid


--- Sentence/switch
id: 53
r#  11027  212  42


--- Sentence//end
id: 53
r#  11278  217  21
p: .
t. 
|   There are,
|  however, detailed features and annotations that may be
|  applied to help grid components interoperate, and to
|  increase overall usability, especially with an
|  emphasis on FAIR (Findable, Accessible, Interoperable,
.  Reusable) priorities


--- Sentence/switch
id: 54
r#  11279  217  22


--- Sentence//end
id: 54
r#  11479  220  55
p: .
t. 
|   One rationale for grid computing
|  is to provide remote services for investigations that
|  require special-purpose equipment  -  i.e., computing
.  power beyond that typically accessible for researchers


--- Sentence/switch
id: 55
r#  11480  220  56


--- Sentence//end
id: 55
r#  11726  224  60
p: .
t. 
|   However, a different
|  aspect of grid architecture is effectively the
|  opposite: allowing scientists and students to replicate
|  research (perhaps in a scaled-down version) on their own
.  computers, or otherwise in quotidian computing environments


--- Sentence/switch
id: 56
r#  11727  224  61


--- Sentence//end
id: 56
r#  11880  227  25
p: .
t. 
|  This serves both data-transparency and pedagogical goals
|  (a concrete forum for fellow investigators to understand published
.  results more thoroughly)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 57
r#  11881  227  26


--- Sentence//end
id: 57
r#  12014  229  47
p: .
t. 
|   For this use-case, it is important
|  for executable research objects to be developed
.  which do not need special-purpose hardware

--- Sentence/end 
id: 57
r#  12015  229  48

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 22
y: N/A


--- Paragraph/start
id: 23
r#  12017  231  1


--- Sentence/start
id: 58
r#  12017  231  1

--- Sentence//end
id: 58
r#  12140  233  26
p: .
t. 
|  There are many advanced science applications that
|  are designed in a self-contained manner, with
.  few external dependencies


--- Sentence/switch
id: 59
r#  12141  233  27


--- Sentence//end
id: 59
r#  12254  235  40
p: .
t. 
|   These can be compiled
|  and executed on ordinary desktop computers, and
.  can form the basis of FAIR data sets


--- Sentence/switch
id: 60
r#  12255  235  41


--- Sentence//end
id: 60
r#  12609  242  41
p: .
t. 
|  It is possible to design executable research objects
|  wherein data visualization and analysis tools are
|  provided as source code files internal to the
|  data set, perhaps by including (in whole or in part)
|  source-code archives for relevant discipline-specific
|  applications, to be compiled on-site (in lieu of
.  downloading prebuilt binary executables)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 61
r#  12610  242  42


--- Sentence//end
id: 61
r#  12792  245  62
p: .
t. 
|   These
|  data packages can likewise contain customized
|  PDF viewers, and the relevant PDF and scientific software
.  may be integrated together for indexing and cross-referencing

--- Sentence/end 
id: 61
r#  12793  245  63

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 23
y: N/A


--- Paragraph/start
id: 24
r#  12797  247  1


--- Sentence/start
id: 62
r#  12797  247  1

--- Sentence//end
id: 62
r#  13003  251  9
p: .
t. 
|  The resulting Research Object model is based on
|  code packages distributed as source files with
|  minimal external dependencies, rather than as binary
|  components or as assets that require external
.  software


--- Sentence/switch
id: 63
r#  13004  251  10


--- Sentence//end
id: 63
r#  13086  252  48
p: .
t. 
|   This paradigm is different than
.  how Research Objects are typically disseminated


--- Sentence/switch
id: 64
r#  13087  252  49


--- Sentence//end
id: 64
r#  13336  257  58
p: .
